  quick_overview = 'An adaptogenic herb traditionally used in Ayurvedic medicine. Modern research supports its role in stress management and promoting a balanced stress response.',
  extended_overview = 'Ashwagandha (Withania somnifera) has been used for over 3,000 years in traditional Ayurvedic medicine, where it is classified as a Rasayana—an herb that promotes longevity and vitality. The name comes from Sanskrit, meaning ''smell of the horse,'' referring both to its distinct aroma and the traditional belief that it imparts the strength and vigor of a horse. Modern scientific research has validated many traditional uses, with clinical trials demonstrating its ability to modulate the body''s stress response through multiple pathways. The root contains bioactive compounds called withanolides, which are believed to be responsible for most of the herb''s therapeutic effects. These compounds have been shown to influence cortisol levels, support healthy neurotransmitter function, and promote overall physiological balance during times of stress.',
  science_snapshot = 'Clinical studies have shown that ashwagandha root extract can significantly reduce cortisol levels and perceived stress scores compared to placebo. Research also suggests potential benefits for sleep quality, cognitive function, and physical performance, though the strength of evidence varies across these applications.',
